    bake_scale: Real3Like,
    bake_transform: TransformRT,
) -> ShapeHandle:
    """Load a shape from a file of supported format, where the file has already been
    loaded into memory, and baking in some scale, rotation, and translation.

    Supported formats: JSON (.mochi.json) and HDF5 (.mochi.h5), auto-detected from
    header bytes. To load a surface mesh format (OBJ, PLY, OFF, STL) from memory,
    use the overload that takes an explicit :class:`~superdex.physics.MeshFileType`.

    Args:
        file_data (ArrayLikeChar): File contents as an in-memory byte array.
        bake_scale (Real3Like): Scale to bake into the shape or (1, 1, 1) for no
            change. Negative scale results in mirroring.
        bake_transform (TransformRT): Transform to bake into the shape, or the
            identity transform for no change.

    Returns:
        A :class:`~superdex.physics.ShapeHandle` that can be used to create actors.

    Raises:
        :class:`~superdex.physics.Error`: If an error occurs.

    Note:
        The shape remains in memory while referenced by any handle or actor. In the
        C API, you must release the shape explicitly by calling
        :func:`~superdex.physics.release_shape`. In C++ and Python, this is
        optional, since :class:`~superdex.physics.ShapeHandle` cleanup is automatic.

    Note:
        Some shape data cannot bake arbitrary non-uniform scale. Unsupported scale
        values are rejected.

    Note:
        Precomputed grid SDF data is preserved only when ``bake_scale`` is uniform
        by absolute value. Non-uniform scale by absolute value discards the
        precomputed SDF. If an SDF collider later requires SDF data, SuperDex
        Physics regenerates it from the transformed mesh at runtime, which may be
        expensive.

    Note:
        If the loaded model has
        :attr:`~superdex.physics.ModelData.element_frame_axes`, ``bake_scale`` and
        ``bake_transform`` transform those axes as normal directions using the
        inverse-transpose of the scale-rotation transform, then normalize them.

    Note:
        Call on any thread.

    See Also:
        :class:`~superdex.physics.ShapeHandle`,
        :func:`~superdex.physics.release_shape`,
        :func:`~superdex.physics.load_shape_from_file`
    """

@overload
